}}{{-}}

Bechtel Jacobs was contracted to dismantle and demolish the K-25 facility in September 2008. The contract, valued at $1.48&nbsp;billion, was made retrospective to October 2007<ref>{{cite news |newspaper=[[Knoxville News Sentinel]] |first1=Frank |last1=Munger |date=24 September 2008 |title=DOE and Bechtel Jacobs sign $1.48B cleanup contract | url=http://www.knoxnews.com/news/2008/Sep/24/092408WebBJC/ |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20140301020544/http://www.knoxnews.com/news/2008/Sep/24/092408WebBJC/ |archive-date=1 March 2014 | access-date=14 February 2009 }}</ref> and ended in August 2011. Demolition work was then carried out by URS | [[CH2M Hill]] Oak Ridge.<ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.ucor.com/_docs/factsheets/ettp_fact_sheet.pdf |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160804060025/http://www.ucor.com/_docs/factsheets/ettp_fact_sheet.pdf |archive-date=4 August 2016 |title=East Tennessee Technology Park Fact Sheet |publisher=DOE Oak Ridge Environmental Management Program |access-date=29 August 2013}}</ref> Demolition was completed in March 2014 <ref>{{cite web |title=Final load of debris shipped from K-25 Building demolition project |date=11 March 2014 |publisher=Department of Energy |url=https://www.energy.gov/orem/articles/final-load-debris-shipped-k-25-building-demolition-project |access-date=1 July 2024}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |publisher=Oak Ridge Today |url=http://oakridgetoday.com/2013/12/19/doe-ucor-demolish-last-piece-k-25-worlds-largest-building |title=DOE, UCOR demolish last piece of K-25, once the world's largest building |date=19 December 2013 |access-date= 19 January 2014}}</ref> Demolition of K-27, the last of the five gaseous diffusion facilities at Oak Ridge, began in February 2016.<ref>{{cite web |url=http://energy.gov/orem/articles/k-27-demolition-will-fulfill-doe-s-vision-2016 |title=K-27 Demolition Will Fulfill DOE's Vision 2016 |date=8 February 2016 |publisher=Department of Energy |access-date=7 June 2016 }}</ref> [[US Senator]] [[Lamar Alexander]] and [[US Congressman]] [[Chuck Fleischmann]] joined 1,500 workers to watch the final wall come down on 30 August 2016. Its demolition was completed in February 2017.<ref>{{cite web |url=https://energy.gov/em/articles/em-marks-another-building-demolition-oak-ridge |title=EM Marks Another Building Demolition at Oak Ridge |date=28 February 2017 |publisher=Department of Energy |access-date=27 May 2017}}</ref> Since 2020, the K-25 site is being [[Redevelopment|redeveloped]] in part into a [[general aviation]] [[airport]] to service the city of Oak Ridge.<ref name="pounds">{{cite news |last1=Pounds |first1=Benjamin |title=More steps taken toward creation of new airport |url=https://www.tennessean.com/story/news/2021/03/10/more-steps-taken-toward-creation-new-airport/6949075002/ |access-date=5 April 2021 |newspaper=[[The Tennessean]] |date=10 March 2021}}</ref> Several small private nuclear facilities are also planned on the site.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Silas |first1=Sloan |title=US nuclear fuel manufacturer will open $13 million production facility in Oak Ridge |url=https://www.knoxnews.com/story/money/2022/03/02/ultra-safe-nuclear-fuel-company-build-oak-ridge-manufacturing-site/9341121002/ |newspaper=[[Knoxville News Sentinel]] |access-date=2 March 2022}}</ref><ref>{{cite news|last1=Benjamin |first1=Pounds |title=Making plans, hiring people for the Hermes reactor |url=https://www.oakridger.com/story/news/2021/10/05/making-plans-hiring-people-hermes-reactor/5909030001/ |newspaper=[[The Oak Ridger]] |access-date=3 March 2022}}</ref><ref>{{cite news|last1=Crocker |first1=Brittany |title=Oak Ridge: Medical isotope producer to be built on decontaminated land near old uranium production plant |url=https://www.knoxnews.com/story/news/2019/04/12/coqui-pharma-production-plant-oak-ridge-tn/3433332002/ |newspaper=[[Knoxville News Sentinel]] |access-date=3 March 2022}}</ref>

==Commemoration==
On 27 February 2020, the K-25 History Center, a 7,500-square foot museum opened at the site. The museum is a branch of the [[American Museum of Science and Energy]] and features hundreds of original artifacts and interactive exhibits related to the K-25 site.<ref>{{cite web |title=Oak Ridge Opens K-25 History Center to Preserve Site's Rich History  |publisher=Department of Energy |url=https://www.energy.gov/orem/articles/oak-ridge-opens-k-25-history-center-preserve-sites-rich-history |access-date=10 December 2022}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |title=K-25 History Center - American Museum of Science and Energy |publisher=American Museum of Science and Energy |url=https://amse.org/k-25-history-center/ |access-date=10 December 2022}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |title=K-25 History Museum - Stay on the Job. Finish the Job |publisher=K-25 History Center |url=https://k25historycenter.org/ |access-date=10 December 2022}}</ref>

